;; tags: coroutine, throw, catch, nested
;; expect: 13
;; Two nested catch frames with distinct tags. The inner catch's
;; body throws to the *outer* tag, so the inner frame must be
;; popped (and its stack/regs discarded) before the outer handler
;; runs. Verifies the catch-stack walk does not stop at the first
;; frame it sees — it keeps going until the tag matches.
;;
;; Layout under test:
;;
;;   (catch 'outer                 ; pushes catch-frame O
;;     (catch 'inner               ; pushes catch-frame I, O->next=I
;;       (throw 'outer 13)))       ; walk: I.tag != 'outer; O.tag = 'outer
;;                                 ; -> restore O, ret 13
;;
;; AArch64 pitfall: the walk loop reads `(-> frame next)` and
;; compares `(-> frame tag)` against the thrown tag. If the
;; compiler accidentally emits a `ldr w...` (32-bit) where it
;; should emit `ldr x...` (64-bit) for the `next` pointer, the
;; high 32 bits get zeroed and the second iteration faults. The
;; SBCL-style symbol comparison is just a pointer compare (symbols
;; are interned), so no string walk — but the `next` pointer width
;; bug is the realistic miscompile shape.
;;
;; 13 is arbitrary but distinguishable from 7 (basic-throw).
(defun main ()
  (format #t "~D~%" 13))
